(a) A bar, as defined in this chapter, not meeting the definition of an eating establishment as defined herein, shall have a minimum seating capacity for not less than 30 persons, as certified by the fire marshal under applicable building, fire and life safety codes, with all seating provided at tables, booths, bars, and counters unobstructed and open to view. No screens, blinds, curtains, partitions, articles, or things may obstruct the inside view of tables, booths, or other areas where customers are served and/or sit; provided that nothing herein shall be interpreted to prevent the licensee from placing curtains, blinds, tint, or other similar dressing on or over windows, except as limited by building or fire code.
(b) Prior to opening and upon annual renewal, a bar shall obtain a certificate of occupancy, certificate of completion, or planning and zoning certification, as applicable, certifying compliance with all applicable building and construction codes and regulations and certifying compliance with fire and life safety codes for the use, occupancy type, and capacity of the establishment.
(c) It shall be the duty of the licensee to strictly enforce occupancy limits at all times. Bar licensees or their designee, such as a bouncer, must provide the current number of occupants upon demand of the fire marshal. Licensees are encouraged to utilize 'fire marshal clickers' or other mechanisms to keep track of the occupancy within the establishment. The fire marshal has authority to determine compliance with occupancy limits based on a visual inspection of the premises and may recommend adverse action against the licensee, including suspension, revocation, or sanction, if the occupancy limit is violated.
(d) All establishments licensed as bars shall be required to have a bouncer, qualified pursuant to the requirements of state law, who shall be primarily responsible for verifying age for admittance, security, maintaining order, or safety, or a combination thereof. In the event that the licensee is required, as a condition of the probationary issuance of the license, to retain an off duty City of Griffin POST certified officer or an off-duty Spalding County Sherriff's Deputy to provide additional security, such officer/deputy shall not be a substitute for a bouncer and shall not be responsible for verifying age of admittance or keeping up with the current occupancy of the establishment.
(e) All establishments licensed as bars shall be required to install and maintain in proper working order security cameras in accordance with this section.
(1) To meet the requirements of this section, cameras must be capable of producing a retrievable image on digital media that can be made a permanent record and can be enlarged.
